        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "1a. Recycling—the practice of separating from ordinary trash specific materials that can be made into new products—is so habitual that many people try to recycle things that aren't actually recyclable: plastic bags, paper coffee cups, Styrofoam, greasy pizza boxes, and more. Such “aspirational recycling” is drawing the attention of waste management professionals, policy makers, and environmental advocates alike.",
            "block_bbox": [
                114,
                259,
                743,
                407
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 4,
            "global_block_id": 4,
            "global_group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    114.0,
                    259.0
                ],
                [
                    743.0,
                    259.0
                ],
                [
                    743.0,
                    407.0
                ],
                [
                    114.0,
                    407.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "That introduction offers a topic, aspirational recycling, but does not motivate us to care about it. Unless a reader is already interested in the issue, she may shrug and ask So what? Why should I care that people try to recycle stuff they can't?",
            "block_bbox": [
                78,
                422,
                773,
                523
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 5,
            "global_block_id": 5,
            "global_group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    78.0,
                    422.0
                ],
                [
                    773.0,
                    422.0
                ],
                [
                    773.0,
                    523.0
                ],
                [
                    78.0,
                    523.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Contrast that introduction with this one. It tells us why aspirational recycling is a problem worth our attention:",
            "block_bbox": [
                79,
                526,
                772,
                577
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 6,
            "global_block_id": 6,
            "global_group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    79.0,
                    526.0
                ],
                [
                    772.0,
                    526.0
                ],
                [
                    772.0,
                    577.0
                ],
                [
                    79.0,
                    577.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "1b. Recycling—the practice of separating from ordinary trash specific materials that can be made into new products—is today an expected and even habitual behavior: to toss an aluminium can in an ordinary trash bin is as unthinkable as it would be to toss it into the street or a neighbor's yard. But many people, out of ignorance or enthusiasm, try to recycle things that aren't actually recyclable: plastic bags, paper coffee cups, Styrofoam, greasy pizza boxes, and more. Such \"aspirational recycling\" has major environmental consequences. By making waste collected for recycling more difficult or even impossible to process, it can result in whole loads of waste intended for recycling going to the landfill instead. We can address the causes of aspirational recycling through better education and better labeling of recycling bins. Thanks to the educational campaigns of the past, people know they should recycle. Now, we need to build on this success by creating more knowledgeable and discriminating recyclers. To this end, recycling bins should be labeled not just with the familiar triangle of arrows that is recycling's universal symbol, but with specific information about what kinds of things can—and can't—be recycled. Pilot programs in several municipalities show that this simple step can reduce aspirational recycling significantly.",
            "block_bbox": [
                109,
                589,
                744,
                1015
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 7,
            "global_block_id": 7,
            "global_group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
